# BrowserX — line endings, so a script behaves the same whoever clones it.
#
# Windows batch has to be CRLF: cmd.exe treats a bare LF as part of the command in
# some constructs, which turns a working script into "The syntax of the command is
# incorrect". Shell scripts and every source file stay LF, so a shebang is never read
# as '/bin/sh\r' ("bad interpreter: No such file or directory"), and the tools that
# read these files (make, python, g++) keep seeing plain Unix endings.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cc    text eol=lf
*.h     text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
